Aim
To implement and compare Logistic Regression, Random Forest, and Gradient Boosting classification algorithms using the Breast Cancer Wisconsin dataset.
Dataset
- Samples: 569
- Features: 30
- Classes: Malignant (0) and Benign (1)
Algorithms Used
1. Logistic Regression: Used for binary classification.
2. Random Forest: Uses multiple decision trees to make predictions.
3. Gradient Boosting: Builds trees one after another to improve prediction.
Procedure
1. Load the Breast Cancer dataset.
2. Divide the dataset into 80% training and 20% testing data.
3. Standardize the features for Logistic Regression.
4. Train all three models.
5. Predict the test data.
6. Calculate Accuracy, Precision, Recall, F1-Score, and Training Time.
7. Compare the performance of all models.
8. Test Random Forest with 10, 50, 100, and 200 trees.
Performance Metrics
- Accuracy: Measures overall correct predictions.
- Precision: Measures how many predicted malignant cases are actually malignant.
- Recall: Measures how many actual malignant cases are correctly detected.
- F1-Score: Combination of Precision and Recall.
- Training Time: Time required to train the model.
Observation
The performance of all three models is compared using graphs and a results table. Random Forest is also analyzed to see how increasing the number of trees affects accuracy and training time.
Result
The three machine learning algorithms were successfully implemented and compared. The model with the highest malignant recall is identified as the best model for detecting malignant cases.
